<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Au Lac</h1><p class="st-meta">257 BCE – 179 BCE (approx.) · capital Co Loa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived kingdom of the Red River delta, famous for the spiral citadel of Co Loa and the legend of King An Duong's magic crossbow.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Au Lac was formed around 257 BCE when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-duong-vuong">Thuc Phan</a>, a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/au-viet">Au Viet</a> people from the northern uplands, overthrew the last Hung king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/van-lang">Van Lang</a> and joined the two peoples under his rule as King An Duong. He built his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/co-loa-citadel">Co Loa</a>, north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/co-loa-citadel">Co Loa</a>, with three concentric ramparts and moats enclosing about 600 hectares, is the largest pre-Han fortified site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and excavations there have found thousands of bronze arrowheads. The kingdom rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture">Dong Son</a> bronze working, and the Lac lords of the older order seem to have kept local power under the new king.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 179 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanyue">Nanyue</a>, conquered Au Lac; legend says his son married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-duong-vuong">An Duong</a>'s daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-chau">My Chau</a> and stole the secret of the magic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbow</a>. The land was absorbed into Nanyue, and after 111 BCE into the Han empire, beginning a long era of Chinese rule.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/au-lac.jpg" alt="Au Lac" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: I Love Triệu Đà · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B%E1%BA%A3n_%C4%91%E1%BB%93_V%C4%83n_Lang_%26_Nam_C%C6%B0%C6%A1ng.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>257 BCE – 179 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Co Loa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>An Duong Vuong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancestor and spirit worship</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Proto-Vietic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Âu Lạc, Thuc dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-218&amp;focus=au-lac" aria-label="Au Lac on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7526 1397 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Au Lac lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7846 1653L7833 1647"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7833 1647h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7846 1653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7846 1653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-218&amp;focus=au-lac">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
